# Expectation record for 05_pointers/006_function_pointer_tables.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 006_function_pointer_tables
area               = 05_pointers
description        = Dispatch through arrays of function pointers, including a two-dimensional table, with a volatile index
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every table entry is initialized with a function of exactly the table's element type, so
every indirect call goes through a compatible type. Every index used is inside its table:
bin_table has 6 entries indexed 0..5, un_table and nil_table have 3 indexed 0..2, and grid
is 2 by 3 indexed [0..1][0..2]. No table entry is null, so no null function pointer is ever
called. All arithmetic inside the called functions is on small values well inside int
range; the largest product computed is 320. The tables are const and are never written.
No signed integer overflow, no shift out of range, no aliasing violation, no read of
uninitialized storage, no object modified twice between sequence points, at most one
side-effecting argument per call, and no dependence on padding bytes or on the relative
addresses of unrelated objects. The only external symbol is printf; no header is included,
no file, socket, environment variable, clock or random source is touched, and every input
is a literal in this source file.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
No function address and no table address is printed. Table facts appear only as equality
comparisons against function designators, as index differences cast to long long, and as
the results of the calls. The two-variant equivalence line compares the runtime sweep
against the folded sweep computed in the same run rather than against a hard-coded
constant, so it cannot drift.
Every index difference described above is cast to long long before printing, so a 32-bit
ptrdiff_t on i686 and a 64-bit ptrdiff_t elsewhere render identically. Plain char is never
printed in a signedness-dependent way. The only conversion specifiers this program uses are
%d and %lld, both of which are target-width independent.
END
